---
interface Stat {
  value: string;
  label: string;
}

interface Props {
  stats: Stat[];
}

const { stats } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="stats-bar" aria-label="Key metrics">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="stats-grid">
      {
        stats.map((stat) => (
          <div class="stat-item">
            <div class="stat-value brutal-stat__value">{stat.value}</div>
            <div class="stat-label brutal-stat__label">{stat.label}</div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .stats-bar {
    background: var(--color-primary);
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* minmax(0, 1fr), not 1fr: a bare 1fr track is minmax(auto, 1fr), so each
     column is floored at its own min-content and the grid can grow past its
     container rather than letting a label wrap. At 320px that put the second
     column's edge 13px off-screen in WebKit — whose text runs wider than
     Chromium's, which is why only one engine showed it. */
  .stats-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0;
  }

  .stat-item {
    text-align: center;
    padding: var(--spacing-2xl);
  }

  .stat-value {
    font-size: 3.5rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    color: light-dark(var(--bg-dark), var(--bg-light));
    line-height: 1;
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm);
  }

  .stat-label {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: light-dark(var(--bg-dark), var(--bg-light));
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.15em;
  }

  /* These steps key on the CONTAINER, not the viewport, because this component
     is rendered at two different widths for the same viewport: `/ma-portfolio/`
     gives it the full `.container`, while the `/brand` specimen frame gives it
     the narrower content column. Viewport queries got the specimen wrong at
     every desktop width — at 1280px the specimen's cells are 218px wide where
     the real page's are 296px, so `$2.8B` hung 14px outside its cell on the page
     that exists to show the component rendering correctly.

     WHERE THE TWO RENDERINGS ACTUALLY DIVERGE (measured 2026-09-01, both routes,
     14 widths): only at 769px and up, where /brand's layout goes two-column and
     the specimen drops to a 361px box while the page holds 673px. At 768px and
     below /brand renders the specimen full-width inside an ordinary page
     `.container`, so BOTH routes get exactly `viewport - gutter` and step tiers
     at the same widths. The divergence is a desktop phenomenon; do not assume
     the specimen is insulated from a page-level layout change at phone widths.

     Where the thresholds come from: the longest value a real page passes is
     `$2.8B+`, six characters, and the pinned face (BL-144) sets six characters
     at 3.6 x the font size. A cell interior is `container / columns - 2 x
     padding`, and each tier is sized so that interior clears the text by AT
     LEAST 8px.

     That 8px floor is the correction, and it is not decoration. The first cut
     of this block solved each threshold as an equality and took whatever slack
     fell out: four columns "cleared" a 1064px container by 0.39px (202px of
     interior against 201.61px of text). It passed locally — `scrollWidth`
     rounds 201.61 up to 202, so the two tie — and failed in CI, where the same
     assertion reported 2px of overflow. Sub-pixel rounding is not a margin.
     8px also absorbs the widest realistic fallback: generic `monospace` on
     Linux is DejaVu at 602/1000, 0.7px wider over six characters than the
     pinned face's 600/1000.

     So, per tier (text = 6 x 0.6 x font-size):
       4 cols, 3.5rem (201.6px), 2rem sides -> c/4 - 64 >= 209.6 -> c >= 1095
       2 cols, 3.5rem (201.6px), 2rem sides -> clears from c = 673 up
       2 cols, 2.5rem (144px),   1rem sides -> c/2 - 32 >= 152 -> c >= 368
       2 cols, 1.5rem (86.4px),  0.5rem sides -> c/2 - 16 >= 94.4 -> c >= 221

     The middle tier's side padding steps down WITH the type for that reason:
     at 2rem sides it needed a 416px container, but it inherits down to 384,
     so containers 385-415 overflowed by up to 15px. (That band was viewport
     481-511 when it was found, under the flat 96px gutter this file used to
     assume — a historical figure, not a current mapping. See below.) No sampled
     width sat in that 30px band — the E2E widths step 480 -> 540 straight over
     it — which is why that file now sweeps rather than samples.

     A THRESHOLD HERE IS A CONTAINER FACT, NOT A VIEWPORT TIER. This block used
     to describe 672 and 384 as "the 768 and 480 viewport tiers expressed as the
     container widths they produce (viewport - 96px)", which was true only while
     `.container` paid a flat 96px at every width. It no longer does: the gutter
     ladder in global.css (3rem / 1.5rem / 1rem) went live on 2026-09-01, and it
     makes viewport -> container DISCONTINUOUS — a wider viewport can produce a
     NARROWER container across a breakpoint. So no container number can express a
     viewport tier any more, and none of these three thresholds tries to.

     They were re-derived when the ladder landed, as the old note demanded, and
     the answer was that all three stay put: leaving them produces only tier
     UPGRADES, on both routes, at every width measured. Moving them to chase the
     old viewport tiers would have downgraded one rendering or the other. The
     mapping table, the discontinuity, and the rejected alternative are in
     ADR-0027 — read that before touching a number here. */
  .container {
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  @container (max-width: 1094px) {
    .stats-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  @container (max-width: 672px) {
    /* The sides step down with the type. At 2rem they need a 416px container,
       but this tier inherits down to 384. (That was the 481-511px viewport band
       when the overflow was found, under the pre-2026-09-01 flat gutter —
       historical, not a current mapping. See ADR-0027.) */
    .stat-item {
      padding: var(--spacing-2xl) var(--spacing-lg);
    }

    .stat-value {
      font-size: 2.5rem;
    }

    .stat-label {
      font-size: var(--text-xs);
    }
  }

  @container (max-width: 384px) {
    /* Two columns rather than stacking to one, which would make four stats four
       screens tall on the narrowest phone. The sizing was derived at a 224px
       container (a 320px viewport under the old flat 96px gutter): a 112px cell,
       six characters at --text-2xl needing 86.4px, so 0.5rem sides left 96px of
       interior and 9.6px of slack. Since the gutter ladder landed (2026-09-01) a
       320px viewport gives a 288px container, so those figures are the tight
       CASE THAT NO LONGER OCCURS — the cell is now 144px and the interior 128px.
       The tier is kept as-is because it is what a genuinely narrow container
       needs, and this component is also rendered in a specimen frame that can be
       narrower than any viewport. The label's tracking steps with it:
       `Engagements` is its longest unbreakable word and 0.15em put it 3px past
       the cell. */
    .stat-item {
      padding: var(--spacing-1_75) var(--spacing-sm);
    }

    .stat-value {
      font-size: var(--text-2xl);
      margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xs);
    }

    /* `Engagements` is 11 characters, and tracking is charged per character:
       at --text-xs it is 79.2px of glyph plus 11 x the tracking. Against the
       96px interior this was derived at, 0.1em cleared by only 3.6px — not an
       overflow, which is why the old clamped assertion passed it, but under the
       floor the sweep now demands. 0.06em costs 7.92px instead of 13.2px and
       cleared by 8.9px, while still reading as tracked uppercase.

       That 96px interior is no longer the narrowest a real viewport produces
       (the gutter ladder makes it 128px at 320px, where 0.1em would clear by
       ~35px), so this is now CONSERVATIVE RATHER THAN EXACT. It stays: the tier
       exists for genuinely narrow containers including the /brand specimen
       frame, and 0.06em is the value that holds at the width the tier is
       actually sized for. */
    .stat-label {
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
  }
</style>
